<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Group Areas Act</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1950</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Group Areas Act of 1950 was a central law of apartheid in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> that assigned separate residential and business areas in towns and cities to each racial group. Enforced over the following decades, it led to the forced removal of hundreds of thousands of people, mainly coloured, Indian and black South Africans, from areas declared white, such as District Six in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-town">Cape Town</a>. It was repealed in 1991.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/group-areas-act.jpg" alt="Group Areas Act" width="320" height="243" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Photo Division, Government of India · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mass_Meeting_at_Durban_on_May_28,_1950_to_protest_against_Group_Area_Bill_and_Suppression_of_Communism_Bill.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
